        "math_academy_id": "q-37067",
        "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{2{x}^{2}+5x+2}.$",
        "worked_solution": "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{2{x}^{2}+5x+2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+4x+x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{2x(x+2)+(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+2)}(x-2)}{(2x+1)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n2x+1=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
